{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"55479615142e6509e73849bdda95ed5ca498ccbb4ff60989bee822cb6e0bd027","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_fb4331d897d593d232f2fc827dc9654306cfc2bd6da0cda5583f15bc790fe307","overall_assessment":"The paper addresses a significant gap in the theoretical analysis of EXP-based algorithms by extending regret bounds to unbounded Gaussian rewards, a setting more reflective of real-world RL than classical bounded assumptions. The proposed EXP4-RL algorithm demonstrates a novel approach to global exploration by combining multiple experts, showing consistent improvements over the RND baseline on hard-to-explore benchmarks. The work contributes both unique theoretical insights and a practical algorithmic framework that advances the understanding of exploration-exploitation trade-offs in reinforcement learning.","paper_id":"-5W5OBfFlwX","paper_identity_sha256":"7bbd39dd95ea5465afe06453d33e829b3685371e3cabe28ca7e158fb06431c3f","prompt_identity_sha256":"659ea5b961f8062699780b24963e8d6b4913f283efa8c906042e0d5596dfca0f","raw_response_sha256":"db6c3350664ae3c187998a95e468d727eb1083425d1a318ced9cff6ac0cf4c46","request_identity_sha256":"e33e3b693dd7df8cc291a6cbbfcb22d9d19ee0296db25ba5311f1e631a0d9edb","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"47860fbf9ca9665e950e1db75e966827fe86a4ef0ebc69d508ec491f5aaed5f1","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-007","SRC-008"],"review_text":"The work addresses a broadly important problem by extending the theoretical analysis of EXP-based algorithms to unbounded Gaussian rewards. This is a significant departure from classical bounded reward assumptions, which limits the applicability of previous results to more realistic reinforcement learning settings. The introduction of Gaussian MAB with unbounded rewards and the derivation of corresponding regret bounds provide a unique theoretical contribution that may influence multiple research areas, including bandit theory and reinforcement learning exploration.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-BRD"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-023","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The paper makes a clear domain-specific contribution to reinforcement learning by proposing EXP4-RL, an algorithm that extends EXP4 to incentivize global exploration in RL. The method is tested on hard-to-explore games such as Montezuma's Revenge and Mountain Car, where it demonstrates superior exploration capabilities compared to the state-of-the-art RND baseline. The use of multiple experts (RND and epsilon-greedy DQN) allows the algorithm to avoid local maxima and achieve more consistent exploration, which is a significant improvement for the RL domain.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-DOM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-022","SRC-032","SRC-035"],"review_text":"The paper reports substantial improvements over the state-of-the-art RND baseline in both theoretical and experimental settings. Theoretically, it establishes upper bounds of order O*(sqrt(T)) for unbounded Gaussian MAB, which is of the same order as bounded MAB but under more general assumptions. Experimentally, EXP4-RL shows consistent improvements in exploration metrics, with relative AUC differences of up to 146% on Montezuma's Revenge and 4.9% on Mountain Car. These results are supported by multiple experimental configurations and demonstrate the robustness of the proposed algorithm.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-SOT"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper discusses the practical implications of its contributions by highlighting the algorithm's ability to improve exploration in hard-to-explore RL environments. The use of multiple experts and the global exploration capability of EXP4-RL have potential real-world benefits for applications requiring robust exploration, such as autonomous systems and game AI. However, the paper does not extensively discuss societal impacts, fairness, or environmental considerations, which are not directly relevant to the core technical contribution but could be relevant in broader deployment contexts.","subdimension_id":"SIGN-IMP"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"1d2b565a375ad7c22c91c98ebb3cb0ffdd32ed349fa184a25846cb7c280c0403","paper_id":"-5W5OBfFlwX","paper_ordinal":106,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"db6c3350664ae3c187998a95e468d727eb1083425d1a318ced9cff6ac0cf4c46","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"-5W5OBfFlwX","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"4fbbca7bc6340e68727e82f0e205af68c1c52886cf5ce7d775760e1acdde4288","request_identity_sha256":"e33e3b693dd7df8cc291a6cbbfcb22d9d19ee0296db25ba5311f1e631a0d9edb","role":"SIGN","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":809,"prompt_tokens":19020,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19829},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"e33e3b693dd7df8cc291a6cbbfcb22d9d19ee0296db25ba5311f1e631a0d9edb","role":"SIGN","role_ordinal":2,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}